1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,820
Salt on your food.

2
00:00:01,820 --> 00:00:03,289
Water in your glass.

3
00:00:03,289 --> 00:00:05,271
A helium balloon at a party.

4
00:00:05,671 --> 00:00:08,980
In salt and water, atoms
hold on to each other.

5
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:10,724
Helium atoms float alone.

6
00:00:10,724 --> 00:00:12,368
They never join anyone.

7
00:00:12,818 --> 00:00:15,818
Last time we asked: why
do atoms bond at all?

8
00:00:15,818 --> 00:00:19,390
Diya asks it again in her
kitchen, holding the salt jar.

9
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:22,216
Welcome to Chapter 4, chemical bonding.

10
00:00:22,216 --> 00:00:24,842
The answer begins with one number: eight.

11
00:00:25,342 --> 00:00:29,111
By the end, you will draw any atom's
outer electrons as dots, and predict

12
00:00:29,111 --> 00:00:30,011
how it bonds.

13
00:00:30,629 --> 00:00:31,933
First, a picture.

14
00:00:31,933 --> 00:00:34,902
Think of a sticker album
page with eight slots.

15
00:00:34,902 --> 00:00:36,698
A full page feels complete.

16
00:00:36,698 --> 00:00:38,191
Seven feels unfinished.

17
00:00:38,541 --> 00:00:40,188
Atoms are a bit like that.

18
00:00:40,188 --> 00:00:44,042
The noble gases, neon and argon,
already have eight electrons in their

19
00:00:44,042 --> 00:00:44,942
outer shell.

20
00:00:45,278 --> 00:00:47,128
Helium is the one exception.

21
00:00:47,128 --> 00:00:49,577
Its only shell is full with just two.

22
00:00:49,977 --> 00:00:52,462
A full outer shell is very stable.

23
00:00:52,462 --> 00:00:55,481
That is why noble gases
almost never react.

24
00:00:56,131 --> 00:00:59,851
In nineteen sixteen, Kossel and Lewis
explained bonding with this idea.

25
00:00:59,851 --> 00:01:01,237
Each worked on his own.

26
00:01:01,587 --> 00:01:04,140
Kossel noticed something
on the periodic table.

27
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:07,572
The noble gases sit between the
halogens and the alkali metals.

28
00:01:07,972 --> 00:01:11,212
Sodium, an alkali metal,
has one outer electron.

29
00:01:11,212 --> 00:01:13,450
Chlorine, a halogen, has seven.

30
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:15,030
Watch the bench.

31
00:01:15,030 --> 00:01:17,292
Sodium gives its one electron away.

32
00:01:17,292 --> 00:01:19,967
Now it has the full outer shell of neon.

33
00:01:19,967 --> 00:01:21,740
It becomes a positive ion.

34
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:23,910
Chlorine takes that electron.

35
00:01:23,910 --> 00:01:25,882
Now it has eight, like argon.

36
00:01:25,882 --> 00:01:27,570
It becomes a negative ion.

37
00:01:27,970 --> 00:01:31,091
Opposite charges pull
together, like two magnets.

38
00:01:31,091 --> 00:01:35,074
This pull is an electrovalent
bond, also called an ionic bond.

39
00:01:35,724 --> 00:01:39,144
Lewis looked at a different
case: two chlorine atoms.

40
00:01:39,144 --> 00:01:41,027
Both want one more electron.

41
00:01:41,027 --> 00:01:42,863
Neither will give one away.

42
00:01:43,263 --> 00:01:44,325
So they share.

43
00:01:44,325 --> 00:01:48,632
Each puts one electron into a shared
pair, and now each counts eight.

44
00:01:48,632 --> 00:01:50,277
This is a covalent bond.

45
00:01:50,927 --> 00:01:51,932
Side by side.

46
00:01:51,932 --> 00:01:54,576
Giving is like passing a toy to a friend.

47
00:01:54,576 --> 00:01:57,779
Sharing is like two friends
holding one toy together.

48
00:01:58,179 --> 00:01:59,262
Pause and think.

49
00:01:59,262 --> 00:02:00,750
Two chlorine atoms bond.

50
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:02,793
Do they give, or do they share?

51
00:02:05,993 --> 00:02:06,893
They share!

52
00:02:06,883 --> 00:02:10,858
Both atoms pull equally hard,
so neither can win the electron.

53
00:02:11,508 --> 00:02:12,751
Now, Lewis symbols.

54
00:02:12,751 --> 00:02:16,774
Lewis drew an atom as a kernel, the
nucleus with the inner electrons, plus

55
00:02:16,774 --> 00:02:18,454
dots for the outer electrons.

56
00:02:18,804 --> 00:02:19,704
Step one.

57
00:02:19,450 --> 00:02:22,639
Count the valence electrons, the
electrons in the outer shell.

58
00:02:22,639 --> 00:02:23,608
Carbon has four.

59
00:02:24,358 --> 00:02:25,258
Step two.

60
00:02:25,039 --> 00:02:28,928
Put one dot on each side of the
symbol: top, right, bottom, left.

61
00:02:29,678 --> 00:02:30,647
Step three.

62
00:02:30,647 --> 00:02:32,639
After four, start pairing.

63
00:02:32,639 --> 00:02:34,368
Nitrogen has five dots.

64
00:02:34,368 --> 00:02:35,337
Oxygen six.

65
00:02:35,337 --> 00:02:36,518
Fluorine seven.

66
00:02:36,518 --> 00:02:37,487
Neon eight.

67
00:02:38,237 --> 00:02:39,137
Step four.

68
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:41,022
The dots tell you the valence.

69
00:02:41,022 --> 00:02:44,880
It is the number of dots, or
eight minus the number of dots.

70
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:46,544
Remember this.

71
00:02:46,544 --> 00:02:50,745
Atoms bond to reach eight outer
electrons, by giving, taking, or sharing.

72
00:02:50,745 --> 00:02:52,197
This is the octet rule.

73
00:02:52,897 --> 00:02:54,725
Now, one worked example.

74
00:02:54,725 --> 00:02:56,338
Magnesium and fluorine.

75
00:02:56,638 --> 00:02:58,644
Magnesium is in group two.

76
00:02:58,644 --> 00:03:00,043
It has two dots.

77
00:03:00,043 --> 00:03:03,545
Fluorine is in group
seventeen, with seven dots.

78
00:03:04,045 --> 00:03:06,386
Magnesium gives away both its electrons.

79
00:03:06,386 --> 00:03:09,766
It becomes M g two plus,
with the outer shell of neon.

80
00:03:10,516 --> 00:03:12,923
One fluorine can take only one electron.

81
00:03:12,923 --> 00:03:14,546
So we need two fluorines.

82
00:03:14,546 --> 00:03:15,935
Each becomes F minus.

83
00:03:16,685 --> 00:03:18,052
Check the charges.

84
00:03:18,052 --> 00:03:21,484
Plus two, plus two times
minus one, gives zero.

85
00:03:21,484 --> 00:03:23,456
The formula is M g F two.

86
00:03:24,206 --> 00:03:27,632
The electrovalence is the
number of charges on the ion.

87
00:03:27,632 --> 00:03:30,020
Two for magnesium, one for fluorine.

88
00:03:30,670 --> 00:03:32,132
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:03:32,482 --> 00:03:35,560
Students write that sodium gains
seven electrons to complete its octet.

90
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:39,128
But losing one is far
easier than gaining seven.

91
00:03:39,128 --> 00:03:42,246
Sodium loses one electron
and becomes N a plus.

92
00:03:42,896 --> 00:03:44,222
Where is this asked?

93
00:03:44,222 --> 00:03:48,199
Expect questions on Lewis symbols,
the octet rule, and the formula and

94
00:03:48,199 --> 00:03:49,605
electrovalence of ions.

95
00:03:50,205 --> 00:03:51,381
Exam level now.

96
00:03:51,381 --> 00:03:53,283
Element X is in group two.

97
00:03:53,283 --> 00:03:55,484
Element Y is in group seventeen.

98
00:03:55,484 --> 00:03:56,758
Which is correct?

99
00:03:56,758 --> 00:03:57,983
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:03,383 --> 00:04:04,804
The answer is A.

101
00:04:04,804 --> 00:04:09,297
The formula is XY two, and the
electrovalence of X is two.

102
00:04:09,797 --> 00:04:12,272
X loses two electrons
and becomes X two plus.

103
00:04:12,772 --> 00:04:16,655
Each Y gains only one electron,
so we need two of them.

104
00:04:17,155 --> 00:04:20,072
Charges: plus two, and
two times minus one.

105
00:04:20,072 --> 00:04:20,972
Zero.

106
00:04:20,602 --> 00:04:21,502
Balanced.

107
00:04:21,921 --> 00:04:23,462
Here is your recap.

108
00:04:23,462 --> 00:04:25,044
Screenshot this card.

109
00:04:28,244 --> 00:04:30,430
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

110
00:04:30,830 --> 00:04:31,730
One.

111
00:04:31,345 --> 00:04:33,940
Why do noble gases almost never react?

112
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:35,640
Two.

113
00:04:35,161 --> 00:04:37,066
Draw the Lewis symbol of nitrogen.

114
00:04:37,866 --> 00:04:38,766
Three.

115
00:04:38,317 --> 00:04:40,439
Which bond forms when
atoms share electrons?

116
00:04:41,339 --> 00:04:45,661
Next time: when sodium meets chlorine,
where does the energy come from

117
00:04:45,661 --> 00:04:46,705
to make salt?

118
00:04:46,705 --> 00:04:49,741
We find out in ionic bond
and lattice enthalpy.
